{
  "name": "Nightly Enpower Path Confirmation checkout with counterparties",
  "today": "Each night between HE 19 and HE 21 the Portland Real Time shift trader checks out the prescheduled power paths with about 27 named marketers and southwest utilities (Northwest counterparties are left to BPA). The trader calls each counterparty, highlights its paths in the Enpower Path Confirmation application, clicks the telephone icon and types the counterparty's contact details into a pop-up. Because the pop-up saves straight to Enpower, each save takes about a minute, during which the application is tied up. Cut paths must also be recorded as confirms, so they go through the same save.",
  "change": "Make the Path Confirmation save run in the background and pre-fill each counterparty's usual contact, so the trader does not sit through a one-minute save after every call.",
  "explanation": "The Enpower team would change the Path Confirmation telephone-icon save so it is queued and written to Enpower in the background (or posted in one batch at the end of the checkout), and would store each counterparty's usual checkout contact so the pop-up opens already filled in. The Real Time shift traders would use it: call the counterparty, highlight its paths, click confirm (changing the contact only if someone else answered) and go straight to the next counterparty. The checkout window stays the same. The estimate counts only the save wait, not the retyping of contacts, and assumes about half of each one-minute wait is truly lost, since the trader can already start dialling the next counterparty while the save runs.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/86.eml",
      "quote": "You must check out with all major marketers and utilities each night."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/86.eml",
      "quote": "Because this information saves directly to Enpower, save time runs about a minute.",
      "note": "Each confirmation save takes about a minute; this is the wait the background save would remove."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/86.eml",
      "quote": "AEP, AES-New Energy, Aquila, Avista, Calpine, Constellation, Coral, Duke, Dynegy, El Paso Merchant, Exelon, Meico, Mirant, Morgan, NCPA, Reliant, PPLMontana, PowerX, Sempra, SMUD, TransAlta, Williams, and southwest utilities such as Pinnacle West, SRP, PNM, PSCO and Pacificorp",
      "note": "27 named counterparties to check out each night, so at least 27 saves a night if each counterparty's paths are confirmed together."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/86.eml",
      "quote": "Suggestion: Sort by counterparty while making confirmation calls. When all schedules with that counterparty are confirmed, highlight each schedule",
      "note": "Paths are grouped and confirmed by counterparty, which supports counting one save per counterparty."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/86.eml",
      "quote": "Confirm a path by highlighting and clicking the telephone icon. A screen will come up requesting contact information from the appropriate counterparty.",
      "note": "The contact details are typed into a pop-up on every confirmation; pre-filling them removes that typing (not counted in the minutes)."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/62.eml",
      "quote": "In the Enpower Path Confirmation, we need to make cuts as Path Confirms.",
      "note": "Cut paths go through the same confirmation save, adding more saves on nights with cuts (not counted)."
    }
  ],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": {
    "count": 1,
    "per": "day",
    "file": "corpus/williams-w3/sent_items/86.eml",
    "quote": "You must check out with all major marketers and utilities each night."
  },
  "minutes": 14
}

{
  "name": "Nightly Enpower Path Confirmation checkout with counterparties",
  "change": "Make the Path Confirmation save run in the background and pre-fill each counterparty's contact, so the Real Time trader does not wait about a minute after each of about 27 nightly confirmations.",
  "reason": "The only evidence is one launch memo (86.eml, 19 Nov 2001), sent two weeks before Enron's bankruptcy and the desk's move to UBS Warburg on new systems, so nothing shows the 27 nightly Enpower confirmations, or the one-minute save, lasting long enough to save 13.5 hours a month; the full minute counted as lost is also not shown, and another critic already rejected this same nightly checkout work on the same evidence.",
  "research": "Find messages from December 2001 onward (Enron estate or UBS Warburg Energy) showing the Real Time desk still confirming paths in Enpower Path Confirmation with many counterparties each night, and any complaint or note about the save time, to show the work and the wait continued."
}

{
  "name": "Monthly roll of Texas transport usage tickets",
  "today": "The desk estimates its transport cost in the P&L during the month from Sitara 'transport usage tickets'. Unify holds the invoicing rates. A usage ticket can only cover about 3 months, so each month someone has to go through every pipe's tickets and add the new month. They type in rates from Lauri Allen's rate matrix and any baseload rates they know of, and leave volume at zero on any month they had to add. In June and July 2000 Heidi Withers did this. It took her parts of two days, and she then asked the schedulers to check volumes on all pipes in Position Manager. Third-party and newly agreed baseload rates are still missing when the tickets roll, so they have to be chased or fixed later. Tickets nobody rolls are found late by schedulers (Midcon, March 2001), who then copy or rebuild them and update MOPS and POPS. Typed rates also drift from the matrix (0.055 on a ticket against 0.05 in the matrix), which throws off the in-month P&L estimate.",
  "change": "Let Sitara transport usage tickets run for the whole contract term, take their rates from the rate matrix automatically, and carry last month's volumes forward, so no one has to roll every ticket by hand each month.",
  "explanation": "Change Sitara so a transport usage ticket can cover the whole contract term (or be marked evergreen) instead of about 3 months. The ticket would point to the rate matrix by pipe, receipt point and delivery point instead of holding a typed rate, so each new matrix month flows through on its own. Only third-party and negotiated baseload rates would be typed in, as overrides. When a new month starts, last month's volume would carry forward instead of being set to zero. Afterwards Heidi Withers' group (or whoever owns the tickets) would type in only the exceptions. Schedulers would change volumes only where flow actually changes, and would no longer find tickets missing for a new month. Ticket rates would match the matrix, so the in-month P&L estimate would not be thrown off by them.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1144.eml",
      "quote": "I updated the June transport usage tickets today through HPL, and will be \nfinishing these tickets tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1257.eml",
      "quote": "I have input and updated transport usage tickets for July."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/2109.eml",
      "quote": "I noticed that the Midcon transport usage tickets at Oakville & Moore are not \nin for March."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1144.eml",
      "quote": "Rule of thumb \nwas that if I had to add a month, I refreshed the rate but left volume at \nzero.",
      "note": "Each roll adds a month, types in the rate again and sets volume to zero. Carrying volume forward and taking the rate from the matrix would remove these steps."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1144.eml",
      "quote": "I would advise that your schedulers check volumes on all pipes to ensure \nthey're reflected in Position Manager correctly.",
      "note": "The roll creates follow-up work for schedulers on every pipe."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1144.eml",
      "quote": "All pipes will be updated by tomorrow morning.",
      "note": "The refresh covers every pipe and takes parts of two working days."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1796.eml",
      "quote": "they can only be set up for approx. 3 months at \na  time.  Therefore, I had to set up 7 usage tickets",
      "note": "The cause of the repeated work: tickets are limited to about 3 months."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/867.eml",
      "quote": "Lauri's matrix shows 0.05 from Thompsonville \nto HSC, but the transport usage ticket (updated by Daren's group) has 0.055.",
      "note": "Typed-in rates drift from the matrix. Taking rates from the matrix would prevent this."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/867.eml",
      "quote": "By not having the right rates on \nthe transport usage tickets, the risk is that the P&L will be off during the \nmonth.",
      "note": "Wrong rates on the tickets throw off the in-month P&L."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/2111.eml",
      "quote": "You can copy the old tickets or create new ones.",
      "note": "A ticket that was not rolled is found late by a scheduler and rebuilt by hand."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1257.eml",
      "quote": "I do not yet know what baseload rates you have agreed upon, so you \nwill need to adjust them in the appropriate tickets.",
      "note": "Negotiated baseload rates would still be typed in by hand. That remaining work is counted in the time after the change."
    }
  ],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": null,
  "minutes": 200
}

