{
  "name": "Settling Real Time CAISO congestion-relief revenue",
  "change": "Take congestion revenue straight from CAPS finals into the daily flash, with an automatic exception list against the inc sheet, instead of trader emails, hand calculations and month-end true-up chases",
  "reason": "Most of the claimed saving rests on misreadings: the largest item (the desk's daily exposure tally, 150 of 370 minutes) is a net-position check the manager asked for and the change would not replace; Jim's hand calculation in 90.eml was a stopgap while the system was down ('When the system comes up'), and he says Volume Management 'haven't missed it for a long time'; the mail shows about 2 events a month, not 4; and once these are corrected, the added daily review of the exception list cancels most of what remains.",
  "research": "Look for evidence that congestion revenue is routinely left out of the flash month after month (true-ups like 266.eml beyond August 2001), and for how long Volume Management actually spends on each event when the system is up, to see whether a narrower check of the flash against CAPS saves real time."
}

{
  "name": "Overriding MIPS zero volumes in POPS by hand",
  "change": "Have MIPS tag incomplete outside-measured volumes as incomplete instead of sending zeros, so POPS keeps Gas Control's estimate and schedulers stop overriding zeros by hand",
  "reason": "The same work and the same change are already saved as item 4 in decisions.md, citing the same five messages, and this version's rate of 2 escalations plus 8 routine overrides a month is not supported by the mail, which shows one confirmed case and one probable case in two weeks of March-April 2000 and nothing after that.",
  "research": null
}

{
  "name": "Setting up a new trading book across TAGG, Sitara, RisktRAC/ERMS, CPR and EOL",
  "change": "One routed new-book request form with status tracking and a single completion notice, instead of separate emails to each system's administrator",
  "reason": "The routed process already exists: Shona Wilson's memo (giron-d/sent/410.eml, Nov 2000) says one RisktRAC book request form goes to Risk Analysis and Reporting, which forwards it to the ERMS administrator and Global Counterparty and sends a final email to the books list, and says this process had 'formally existed for a number of months'; Giron's own requests from Oct 2000 on (557, 564, 491, 160) are single form submissions to Trevino/McIntyre, so the scattered emails cited come from mid-2000, when people were not following the process, not from how the work runs now.",
  "research": "Only a new angle could revive it: mail from after Nov 2000 showing that book requests made through the single form still bounce, stall or need follow-up emails to CPR, EOL or Sitara administrators. The steps the form does not cover (the CPR region and EOL trader mapping) would then be a narrower proposal, with its own count of how often it happens."
}
